WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.828
Common Rust anti-patterns are review
signals.

2
00:00:02.908 --> 00:00:04.596
They do not mean the author failed;

3
00:00:04.676 --> 00:00:08.576
they mean the code is telling the team
where assumptions need to become clearer.

4
00:00:08.656 --> 00:00:11.974
Treat borrow checker friction, repeated
clones, broad lifetimes,

5
00:00:12.054 --> 00:00:14.208
shared ownership, panics, raw primitives,

6
00:00:14.288 --> 00:00:17.548
and broad mutable state as prompts to ask
better design questions.

7
00:00:17.628 --> 00:00:19.781
Prefer clear ownership and narrow borrows.

8
00:00:19.861 --> 00:00:21.841
Use compiler feedback constructively.

9
00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:26.170
Handle expected errors with `Result`,
`Option`, and deliberate recovery decisions.

10
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:30.266
Model important domain concepts with
newtypes, enums, validated constructors,

11
00:00:30.346 --> 00:00:32.907
and APIs that make invalid states harder
to express.

12
00:00:32.987 --> 00:00:36.480
Keep unsafe and FFI boundaries small,
documented, justified, tested,

13
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.957
and wrapped where possible.

14
00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:41.180
Explain safety contracts in terms another
reviewer can verify.

15
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.472
Validate input after parsing and before
use.

16
00:00:43.552 --> 00:00:47.278
Review numeric conversions, indexes,
allocation sizes, resource limits,

17
00:00:47.358 --> 00:00:51.549
and boundary assumptions before they
influence sensitive or expensive operations.

18
00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:53.142
Look beyond the core language.

19
00:00:53.222 --> 00:00:57.355
Async systems need timeouts, cancellation
rules, backpressure, lock discipline,

20
00:00:57.435 --> 00:00:58.541
and cleanup behavior.

21
00:00:58.621 --> 00:01:02.172
Dependencies, feature flags, build
scripts, procedural macros, logs,

22
00:01:02.252 --> 00:01:06.268
metrics, release processes, and deployment
checks are part of the program's real

23
00:01:06.348 --> 00:01:07.279
security posture.

24
00:01:07.359 --> 00:01:08.756
The goal is not perfect code.

25
00:01:08.836 --> 00:01:12.562
The goal is code whose assumptions are
visible enough to review and improve.

26
00:01:12.642 --> 00:01:14.504
Rust gives teams a strong foundation,

27
00:01:14.584 --> 00:01:18.543
and anti-pattern review helps keep that
foundation aligned with maintainable,

28
00:01:18.623 --> 00:01:20.660
secure, production-ready engineering.
